    "lawCode": "Amd §6-136, El L",
    "lawSection": "Election Law",
    "memo": "\n \nBILL NUMBER: S2862\n\nSPONSOR: MAY\n \nTITLE OF BILL:\n\nAn act to amend the election law, in relation to the number of signa-\ntures for designating petitions in the year 2019\n\n \nPURPOSE:\n\nTo ensure ballot access for a variety of candidates for office due to\nthe recent change in the primary election date.\n\n \nSUMMARY OF PROVISIONS:\n\nSection one - adds new subdivisions 5 to § 5-136 of the election law to\nreduce the number of signatures required on designating petitions for\nall public offices to be filled in 2019 with the exception of New York\nCity.\n\n \nJUSTIFICATION:\n\nIn 2009, Congress passed the Military and Overseas Voters Act (MOVE),\nwhich required a number of reforms to ensure military, federal workers\nand overseas voters had the opportunity to cast their ballot in a timely\nmanner. In 2012, US District Judge Gary Sharpe ruled that New York\nState's September primary was in violation of the MOVE Act and subse-\nquently ordered the Federal Primary to be moved to the 4th Tuesday in\nJune, leaving it to the legislature to determine when to hold State and\nlocal primaries. This left New York with two separate primaries: one for\nfederal offices and one for state and local offices.\n\nIn 2019, after 8 years of impasse the legislature has passed legislation\nreunifying state, local and federal (non-presidential) primary elections\nto be held on the 4th Tuesday in June, effective immediately.\n\nHowever, because of the imminent change in the political calendar neces-\nsary to meet the requirements for a June primary, candidates for local\noffices in 2019 are required to begin circulating designating petitions\nat the end of February 2019, rather than in mid-June, 2019.\n\nThis bill would temporarily reduce the number of signatures required for\ndesignating petitions by 1/4 for candidates running for office, with the\nexception of NYC, in 2019 to allow candidates to organize their\ncampaigns, print their petitions and begin collecting voters' signatures\nafter the change in the primary date.\n\n \nPRIOR LEGISLATIVE HISTORY:\n\nThis is a new bill.\n\n \nFISCAL IMPLICATIONS:\n\nNone\n\n \nEFFECTIVE DATE:\nThis act shall take effect immediately and shall expire December 31,\n2019 when upon such date the provisions of this act shall be deemed\nrepealed.\n",
